# Firestore Patterns

**Entry point:** [`AGENTS.md`](../../AGENTS.md) · **Validation:** `@/types/firestoreParse` (Zod) on all reads

## Collections
- `games/{gameId}` → `Game` interface
- `games/{gameId}/players/{uid}` → `Player` interface
- `packs/{packId}` → `TaskPack` (read-only, seeded)
- `missions/{missionId}` → missions with `pack_id`, `difficulty`, `directive` (read-only, seeded)

## Transaction Rules (CRITICAL)
Firestore rejects transactions that interleave reads and writes:

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT: all reads first, then all writes
await runTransaction(db, async (transaction) => {
  const doc1 = await transaction.get(ref1);
  const doc2 = await transaction.get(ref2);
  transaction.update(ref1, { ... });
  transaction.update(ref2, { ... });
});

// ❌ WRONG: read after write
await runTransaction(db, async (transaction) => {
  const doc1 = await transaction.get(ref1);
  transaction.update(ref1, { ... });
  const doc2 = await transaction.get(ref2);  // WILL FAIL
});
```

## Known Data Integrity Issues
- `startGame` uses `writeBatch` not a transaction → race if player joins during setup
- `game.playerIds[]` can drift from the actual `players` subcollection
- No server-side host authorization (client-side only for MVP)

## Adding New Mutations
1. Pure logic goes in `gameLogic.ts` (no Firestore imports)
2. Firestore I/O goes in `gameService.ts`
3. Wrap multi-document writes in `runTransaction` when consistency matters
4. Use `computeEliminationUpdates()` for any elimination-related logic
5. Use `resolveAvailableTasks()` + `pickRandomTask()` for task selection
